{
  "assessment": "Introduces `billing/pricing.py` as a standalone module that calculates line totals using raw floats and defers quantization to `total_of()`. This removes the per-line rounding error accumulation but leaves the new code unconnected to any visible receipt generation pipeline. Without explicit integration, it acts as dead code relative to the existing execution path.",
  "findings": [
    {
      "actual": "No imports or references to `pricing` appear anywhere in this diff; if `run.py` retains its original inline billing logic, this file is never loaded and the bug remains unfixed.",
      "at": "billing/pricing.py",
      "call": "grep -r \"import pricing\" . && grep -r \"from pricing import\" . || echo 'no imports found'",
      "defect": "Dead / Unwired Module","expected": "The receipt generation path must explicitly load or use this module; currently it cannot be reached."
    },
    {
      "actual": "All price arithmetic uses IEEE 754 `float` (e.g., `self.subtotal`, `self.tax`). Base-10 currency values suffer representation drift in binary floats, which can still corrupt intermediate values before the final quantization.",
      "at": "billing/pricing.py",
      "call": "python3 -c \"from billing import pricing; print(repr(0.1 * 3))\"",
      "defect": "Float Precision Vulnerability","expected": "Financial arithmetic should use `decimal.Decimal` from input through calculation to guarantee exact rounding behavior."
    },
    {
      "actual": "`Line.total` returns an unrounded float and only `total_of()` quantizes once. If downstream code formats lines for display using standard rounding (e.g., `.2f`), the sum of displayed values will diverge from the invoice total by multiple pennies.",
      "at": "billing/pricing.py",
      "call": "python3 run.py data/orders.json | grep -A5 'TOTAL'",
      "defect": "Missing Residual Rounding (Big Money)","expected": "To guarantee displayed lines sum exactly to the printed total, a residual allocation algorithm must be applied to one of the line items before printing."
    }
  ]
}
